100 kg (220 lb); BMI is 36 kg/m2 . His temperature is 37°C (98.6°F), pulse is 68/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 150/85 mm Hg. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. On mental status examination, he is tired and has a restricted affect. There is no evidence of suicidal ideation. Cognition is intact. His fasting serum glucose concentration is 120 mg/dL. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management? \n\nA. Insist on hospitalization against the patient's wishes\nB. Initiate insulin therapy immediately\nC. Begin a psychotherapy session\nD. Seek a court order to appoint a legal guardian\nE. Schedule neuropsychological testing\nF. Start a diet and exercise regimen\nG. Prescribe medication for depression\nH. Conduct a full body physical examination\nI. Adhere to the patient's wishes on the condition that he agree to home nursing care\nJ. Adhere to the patient's wishes and discuss home-care options\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "A thirty-seven-year-old man with type 1 diabetes mellitus is admitted to the hospital because of inadequate control of his glucose concentrations for the past two months. Despite his poor control, he demands that he be discharged. He reports that he has had a three-month history of fatigue, irritability, and feelings of hopelessness. He says that he has been noncompliant with his diabetic regimen, adding, \"Sometimes I forget.\" He has been avoiding his family and friends because he is not in the mood to see them, but admits that he is lonely. He did not get out of bed for two days, which prompted his wife to call an ambulance and have him admitted to the hospital. Prior to admission to the hospital, his only medication was insulin, although he often missed doses. He does not drink alcohol. He is 168 centimeters (5 feet 6 inches) tall and weighs 100 kilograms (220 pounds). BMI is 36 kilograms per meter squared. His temperature is 37 degrees Celsius (98.6 Fahrenheit), respirations are 18 per minute, and blood pressure is 150 over 85 millimeters of mercury. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. On mental status examination, he is tired and has a restricted affect. There is no evidence of suicidal ideation. Cognition is intact. His fasting serum glucose concentration is 120 milligrams per deciliter. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management? (A) Insist on hospitalization against the patient's wishes. (B) Initiate insulin therapy immediately. (C) Begin a psychotherapy session. (D) Seek a court order to appoint a legal guardian. (E) Schedule neuropsychological testing. (F) Start a diet and exercise regimen. (G) Prescribe medication for depression. (H) Conduct a full-body physical examination. (I) Adhere to the patient's wishes, on the condition that he agree to home nursing care. (J) Adhere to the patient's wishes and discuss homecare options.", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Given the patient's intact cognition and lack of suicidal ideation, he seems to have decision-making capacity. So, it's best to respect his wishes for discharge while discussing homecare options and planning outpatient support to help manage both his diabetes and depression.", "expert_answer": "**J) Adhere to the patient's wishes and discuss homecare options.**\n\nHe appears to have decision-making capacity: cognition is intact, there is no psychosis or suicidal ideation, and depression alone does not justify involuntary hospitalization. Therefore, his request for discharge should be respected while arranging appropriate outpatient support and follow-up, including home care options and treatment for depression.", "expert_latency_s": 5.92, "stall_ms": 31, "relay_ms": 1540}
{"id": "q0193", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 111.38, "n_chunks": 112, "scores": [0.4045, 0.3736, 0.2915, 0.3222, 0.2638, 0.3014, 0.2928, 0.3167, 0.3351, 0.408, 0.4603, 0.3308, 0.3249, 0.2959, 0.3436, 0.3285, 0.4788, 0.3312, 0.2605, 0.2495, 0.2334, 0.2427, 0.3759, 0.3295, 0.2971, 0.225, 0.2238, 0.3632, 0.2338, 0.1494, 0.282, 0.2992, 0.4495, 0.3111, 0.3447, 0.294, 0.2134, 0.3552, 0.4228, 0.3021, 0.4638, 0.3782, 0.4603, 0.3242, 0.532, 0.5144, 0.3635, 0.3257, 0.3071, 0.4993, 0.4259, 0.3776, 0.3701, 0.3678, 0.396, 0.4001, 0.3038, 0.365, 0.4278, 0.3833, 0.599, 0.4674, 0.474, 0.3453, 0.452, 0.239, 0.2878, 0.373, 0.345, 0.3461, 0.3333, 0.3249, 0.4338, 0.4337, 0.4788, 0.3338, 0.349, 0.427, 0.3296, 0.2903, 0.3614, 0.3608, 0.3434, 0.3833, 0.2375, 0.4147, 0.4205, 0.3916, 0.4558, 0.3225, 0.2522, 0.4888, 0.3889, 0.4627, 0.3494, 0.4133, 0.4623, 0.468, 0.3134, 0.4311, 0.4086, 0.4775, 0.3644, 0.3865, 0.4346, 0.3158, 0.4033, 0.4483, 0.385, 0.274, 0.4836, 0.3981], "eot_score": 0.8328, "eot_read_ms": 26, "reference_answer": "D. The plaintiff must demonstrate that there is no rational relationship between the ordinance and any legitimate state interest, because the ordinance regulates economic activity of a type normally presumed to be within state regulatory authority.", "query": "To improve the quality of rental housing within its boundaries, a city proposed an ordinance requiring all new and existing rental housing units to provide at least one full bathroom for each bedroom, plumbing and electrical hookups for a washer and dryer, and a covered parking space. A majority of the owners of existing rental housing in the city opposed the ordinance. They argued that it would dramatically decrease the number of lowincome rental housing units because owners would be unable to raise rents enough to recoup the investment required to comply. Without denying these contentions, the city enacted the ordinance. A plaintiff who owns low-income rental housing has sued the city, claiming only that the ordinance is unconstitutional on its face. Which of the following best states the burden of persuasion in this action?\n\nA. The city must demonstrate that the ordinance is necessary to serve a compelling state interest, because it adversely affects the fundamental right of rental housing owners to use their property in the manner they deem most economically efficient.\nB. The city must demonstrate that the ordinance is necessary to serve a compelling state interest, because it will have a substantial and disproportionate negative impact on low-income persons.\nC. The plaintiff must demonstrate that the ordinance is not substantially related to an important state interest, because it requires some owners of rental housing to invest money that they will not be able to recoup from increased rents.\nD. The plaintiff must demonstrate that there is no rational relationship between the ordinance and any legitimate state interest, because the ordinance regulates economic activity of a type normally presumed to be within state regulatory authority.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "To improve the quality of rental housing within its boundaries, a city proposed an ordinance requiring all new and existing rental housing units to provide at least one full bathroom for each bedroom, plumbing and electrical hookups for a washer and dryer, and a covered parking space. A majority of the owners of existing rental housing in the city opposed the ordinance. They argued that it would dramatically decrease the number of low income rental housing units because owners would be unable to raise rents enough to recoup the investment required to comply. Without denying these contentions, the city enacted the ordinance. A plaintiff who owns low income rental housing has sued the city, claiming only that the ordinance is unconstitutional on its face. Which of the following best states the burden of persuasion in this action? (A) The city must demonstrate that the ordinance is necessary to serve a compelling state interest because it adversely affects the fundamental right of rental housing owners to use their property in the manner they deem most economically efficient. (B) The city must demonstrate that the ordinance is necessary to serve a compelling state interest because it will have a substantial and disproportionate negative impact on low income persons. (C) The plaintiff must demonstrate that the ordinance is not substantially related to an important state interest because it requires some owners of rental housing to invest money that they will not be able to recoup from increased rents. (D) The plaintiff must demonstrate that there is no rational relationship between the ordinance and any legitimate state interest because the ordinance regulates economic activity of a type normally presumed to be within state regulatory authority.", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The correct answer is D. The plaintiff must show that there's no rational relationship between the ordinance and any legitimate state interest, given that it regulates economic activity typically under state authority.", "expert_answer": "(D) The plaintiff must demonstrate that there is no rational relationship between the ordinance and any legitimate state interest because the ordinance regulates economic activity of a type normally presumed to be within state regulatory authority.", "expert_latency_s": 2.96, "stall_ms": 30, "relay_ms": 1068}
{"id": "q0202", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 84.28, "n_chunks": 85, "scores": [0.4292, 0.3549, 0.3207, 0.3674, 0.4552, 0.4975, 0.4525, 0.33, 0.3338, 0.3466, 0.4156, 0.4374, 0.4419, 0.343, 0.3427, 0.3995, 0.307, 0.3638, 0.3343, 0.4048, 0.3296, 0.3434, 0.3956, 0.4548, 0.295, 0.2847, 0.3029, 0.2427, 0.2977, 0.1638, 0.4216, 0.3271, 0.2923, 0.4113, 0.284, 0.304, 0.3134, 0.3495, 0.319, 0.3448, 0.3325, 0.4203, 0.4055, 0.4302, 0.4931, 0.4829, 0.5771, 0.4852, 0.4491, 0.4522, 0.5503, 0.2917, 0.4484, 0.4945, 0.4897, 0.5308, 0.4537, 0.4572, 0.3827, 0.4245, 0.4739, 0.4868, 0.4409, 0.3635, 0.4028, 0.469, 0.5085, 0.392, 0.4707, 0.4448, 0.4397, 0.3321, 0.3722, 0.342, 0.5391, 0.3869, 0.4758, 0.3946, 0.4455, 0.4294, 0.4316, 0.4097, 0.2936, 0.3981, 0.4896], "eot_score": 0.7585, "eot_read_ms": 28, "reference_answer": "I. the friend only.", "query": "A shopkeeper is the owner of a vacant lot in fee simple absolute (the record title is also in the shopkeeper). In 1960, the shopkeeper conveyed the property by general warranty deed as follows: \"The grantor hereby conveys the vacant lot to my friend, but if the property shall ever be used for church purposes, then to the children of my sister who reach the age of 25. \" At the time of the conveyance, the sister was single. Thereafter, the sister married and had two sons. Subsequently, one son died in an automobile accident. Three years after that son's death, the sister had a daughter. In an appropriate action to determine the ownership rights to the vacant lot with all minors represented, title is in \n\nA. the friend, the surviving son, and the sister.\nB. the friend, the son, the daughter, and any additional children of the sister born within 21 years after the death of the deceased son.\nC. the friend and the surviving son only.\nD. the friend and the sister.\nE. the friend, the deceased son, and the daughter.\nF. the friend, the son, the daughter, and any additional children of the sister born during her lifetime.\nG. the friend, the son, and the daughter.\nH. the sister and her children only.\nI. the friend only.\nJ. the friend and any children of the sister reaching the age of 25.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "A shopkeeper is the owner of a vacant lot in fee simple absolute. The record title is also in the shopkeeper. In 1960, the shopkeeper conveyed the property by general warranty deed as follows: \"The grantor hereby conveys the vacant lot to my friend, but if the property shall ever be used for church purposes, then to the children of my sister who reach the age of 25.\" At the time of the conveyance, the sister was single. Thereafter, the sister married and had two sons. Subsequently, one son died in an automobile accident. Three years after that son's death, the sister had a daughter. In an appropriate action to determine the ownership rights to the vacant lot with all minors represented, title is in (A) the friend, the surviving son, and the sister; (B) the friend, the son, the daughter, and any additional children of the sister born within 21 years after the death of the deceased son; (C) the friend and the surviving son only; (D) the friend and the sister; (E) the friend, the deceased son, and the daughter; (F) the friend, the son, the daughter, and any additional children of the sister born during her lifetime; (G) the friend, the son, and the daughter; (H) the sister and her children only; (I) the friend only; (J) the friend and any children of the sister reaching the age of 25. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The correct answer is I. the friend only.", "expert_answer": "(I) the friend only.", "expert_latency_s": 4.89, "stall_ms": 32, "relay_ms": 327}
{"id": "q0213", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 13.54, "n_chunks": 14, "scores": [0.4259, 0.3396, 0.2338, 0.4339, 0.2903, 0.381, 0.4217, 0.5106, 0.2421, 0.4635, 0.3967, 0.3218, 0.6355, 0.3958], "eot_score": 0.725, "eot_read_ms": 30, "reference_answer": "D. Mongolia", "query": "Which country faces a serious trade disadvantage due to its location?\n\nA. Canada\nB. Japan\nC. Germany\nD. Mongolia\nE. Brazil\nF. Italy\nG. Singapore\nH. Spain\nI. United States\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "D) Mongolia", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Mongolia faces a serious trade disadvantage due to its location.", "expert_answer": "D) Mongolia", "expert_latency_s": 4.78, "stall_ms": 31, "relay_ms": 426}
{"id": "q0221", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 78.79, "n_chunks": 79, "scores": [0.4855, 0.472, 0.3399, 0.3057, 0.3314, 0.3185, 0.3164, 0.3529, 0.2261, 0.2984, 0.3137, 0.2753, 0.2571, 0.314, 0.2688, 0.323, 0.3065, 0.2299, 0.3753, 0.2579, 0.181, 0.4342, 0.3901, 0.3457, 0.1858, 0.4843, 0.3255, 0.3781, 0.4334, 0.4085, 0.2371, 0.4627, 0.3058, 0.5475, 0.4357, 0.3353, 0.6177, 0.2848, 0.3278, 0.3417, 0.2768, 0.3168, 0.3397, 0.447, 0.3913, 0.3734, 0.4198, 0.2917, 0.3734, 0.3486, 0.4525, 0.4415, 0.3149, 0.4494, 0.3113, 0.3198, 0.3677, 0.5168, 0.338, 0.4107, 0.3199, 0.4314, 0.4083, 0.322, 0.4182, 0.2955, 0.4448, 0.3851, 0.3818, 0.4747, 0.382, 0.3641, 0.3665, 0.3621, 0.4792, 0.4743, 0.3327, 0.1957, 0.4038], "eot_score": 0.7203, "eot_read_ms": 27, "reference_answer": "G. admissible, whether or not the son is available as a witness.", "query": "A relevant fact in a plaintiff's suit is the magnitude of an explosion at an oil refinery one evening. At trial, the plaintiff was asked on direct examination if he remembered the explosion. He replied, \"I recall my son running into the home and screaming that the car windows just shattered. \" The son was available to testify at trial. The defendant's attorney makes a motion to strike the plaintiff's answer. The trial judge should rule the plaintiff's testimony\n\nA. admissible, because the son is available as a witness.\nB. admissible, if the son's testimony corroborates the plaintiff's statement.\nC. inadmissible, because the statement can be considered secondhand information.\nD. admissible, because it gives context to the magnitude of the explosion.\nE. inadmissible under the excited utterance exception, because it can be a product of reflection and deliberation.\nF. admissible, because it provides relevant details about the incident.\nG. admissible, whether or not the son is available as a witness.\nH. inadmissible, because the plaintiff's statement is based on the son's account.\nI. inadmissible as hearsay not within any recognized exception.\nJ. inadmissible, unless the son's account is presented as evidence.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "A relevant fact in a plaintiff's suit is the magnitude of an explosion at an oil refinery one evening. At trial, the plaintiff was asked on direct examination if he remembered the explosion. He replied, \"I recall my son running into the home and screaming that the car windows just shattered.\" The son was available to testify at trial. The defendant's attorney makes a motion to strike the plaintiff's answer. The trial judge should rule the plaintiff's testimony (A) admissible because the son is available as a witness; (B) admissible if the son's testimony corroborates the plaintiff's statement; (C) inadmissible because the statement can be considered secondhand information; (D) admissible because it gives context to the magnitude of the explosion; (E) inadmissible under the excited utterance exception because it can be a product of reflection and deliberation; (F) admissible because it provides relevant details about the incident; (G) admissible whether or not the son is available as a witness; (H) inadmissible because the plaintiff's statement is based on the son's account; (I) inadmissible as hearsay not within any recognized exception; (J) inadmissible unless the son's account is presented as evidence. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The correct answer is G. The plaintiff's testimony would be admissible whether or not the son is available as a witness.", "expert_answer": "(G) admissible whether or not the son is available as a witness.", "expert_latency_s": 4.27, "stall_ms": 33, "relay_ms": 811}
{"id": "q0233", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 34.04, "n_chunks": 35, "scores": [0.4245, 0.4339, 0.4716, 0.3625, 0.3018, 0.4985, 0.3769, 0.4545, 0.3783, 0.3539, 0.4825, 0.5037, 0.3779, 0.4418, 0.4389, 0.3246, 0.2703, 0.3923, 0.4967, 0.3373, 0.401, 0.4385, 0.4503, 0.4265, 0.3481, 0.4515, 0.3647, 0.1633, 0.4546, 0.4246, 0.4663, 0.448, 0.4223, 0.5737, 0.4508], "eot_score": 0.6864, "eot_read_ms": 29, "reference_answer": "F. our consciences are fallible.", "query": "Anscombe criticizes Butler on the grounds that:\n\nA. the notion of \"freedom\" is overly simplistic.\nB. the concept of “pleasure” is unclear.\nC. the notion of “legislating for oneself” is absurd.\nD. the idea of \"moral truth\" is a contradiction.\nE. we can't really distinguish between good and evil.\nF. our consciences are fallible.\nG. we really can derive an “ought” from an “is.”\nH. the idea of moral obligations is incoherent.\nI. the concept of \"identity\" is too subjective.\nJ. the concept of \"self\" is ill-defined.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "Anscombe criticizes Butler on the grounds that (a) the notion of freedom is overly simplistic, (b) the concept of pleasure is unclear, (c) the notion of legislating for oneself is absurd, (d) the idea of moral truth is a contradiction, (e) we can't really distinguish between good and evil, (f) our consciences are fallible, (g) we really can derive an ought from an is, (h) the idea of moral obligations is incoherent, (i) the concept of identity is too subjective, (j) the concept of self is ill-defined.", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Anscombe criticizes Butler on the grounds that our consciences are fallible.", "expert_answer": "(f) our consciences are fallible.", "expert_latency_s": 5.56, "stall_ms": 30, "relay_ms": 541}
{"id": "q0239", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 71.14, "n_chunks": 72, "scores": [0.4084, 0.3807, 0.3565, 0.3963, 0.3651, 0.3742, 0.3853, 0.5124, 0.3939, 0.421, 0.3895, 0.4548, 0.3606, 0.3118, 0.4325, 0.5111, 0.3942, 0.2555, 0.3243, 0.3548, 0.436, 0.3421, 0.3487, 0.3647, 0.4941, 0.3531, 0.2287, 0.2723, 0.5997, 0.4724, 0.4365, 0.3623, 0.2872, 0.2771, 0.368, 0.2714, 0.1724, 0.649, 0.1198, 0.2054, 0.2596, 0.4998, 0.3855, 0.3453, 0.3065, 0.2603, 0.3787, 0.4791, 0.5096, 0.3657, 0.4734, 0.4561, 0.6762, 0.4426, 0.4466, 0.3971, 0.4847, 0.5159, 0.5774, 0.4544, 0.4861, 0.5836, 0.5637, 0.4001, 0.4039, 0.4336, 0.4197, 0.7556, 0.4495, 0.1842, 0.5893, 0.55], "eot_score": 0.9641, "eot_read_ms": 30, "reference_answer": "B. 1.92 hr", "query": "A steel cylinder contains liquid at a mean bulk temperature of 80°F. Steam condensing at 212°F on the outside surface is used for heating the liquid. The coefficient of heat transfer on the steam side is 1,000 Btu/hr-ft^2-°F. The liquid is agitated by the stirring action of a turbine impeller. Its diameter is 2 ft., and it moves at an angular velocity of 100 rpm. The cylinder is 6 ft. long, with a diameter of 6 ft. and a wall thickness of 1/8 in. The thermal conductivity of steel may be taken as 9.4 Btu/hr-ft^2-°F. Properties of the liquid, taken as constant, are: c_p = 0.6 Btu/lbm-°Fk = 0.1 Btu/hr-ft-°F \\rho = 60lbm/ft^3 The viscosity at 130°F is 653.4lbm/ft-hr, and at 212°F is 113.74lbm/ft-hr. Calculate the time required to raise the mean bulk temperature of the liquid to 180°F.\n\nA. 3.5 hr\nB. 1.92 hr\nC. 4.0 hr\nD. 1.75 hr\nE. 2.0 hr\nF. 3.0 hr\nG. 2.5 hr\nH. 1.5 hr\nI. 2.75 hr\nJ. 2.15 hr\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "A steel cylinder contains liquid at a mean bulk temperature of 80 degrees Fahrenheit. Steam condensing at 212 degrees Fahrenheit on the outside surface is used for heating the liquid. The coefficient of heat transfer on the steam side is 1000 BTU/hr-ft^2-degF. The liquid is agitated by the stirring action of a turbine impeller. Its diameter is 2 feet, and it moves at an angular velocity of 100 RPM. The cylinder is 6 feet long, with a diameter of 6 feet, and a wall thickness of 1/8 inch. The thermal conductivity of steel may be taken as 9.4 BTU/hr-ft-degF. Properties of the liquid taken as constant are: c_p = 0.6 BTU/lbm-degF, nu = 0.116 x 10^-4 m^2/s. The viscosity at 130 degrees Fahrenheit is 653.4 lbm/ft-hr, and at 212 degrees is 113.74 lbm/ft-hr. Calculate the time required to raise the mean bulk temperature of the liquid to 180 degrees Fahrenheit. A) 3.5 hr B) 1.92 hr C) 4.1 hr D) 1.755 hr E) 2.05 hr F) 2.5 hr G) 1.5 hr H) 2.75 hr I) 2.1 hr. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The time required to raise the mean bulk temperature of the liquid to 180°F is 1.755 hours.", "expert_answer": "D) 1.755 hr", "expert_latency_s": 31.98, "stall_ms": 31, "relay_ms": 846}
{"id": "q0243", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 51.85, "n_chunks": 52, "scores": [0.3134, 0.2342, 0.3654, 0.3004, 0.3467, 0.3299, 0.4018, 0.4534, 0.4302, 0.3688, 0.3885, 0.4196, 0.3951, 0.3339, 0.3224, 0.3854, 0.3472, 0.2661, 0.3778, 0.287, 0.2024, 0.4215, 0.3687, 0.3052, 0.3912, 0.3213, 0.3771, 0.3764, 0.3752, 0.3633, 0.3942, 0.3113, 0.3944, 0.2506, 0.3636, 0.3607, 0.484, 0.4144, 0.4393, 0.5093, 0.3587, 0.3091, 0.284, 0.4127, 0.3743, 0.4154, 0.3655, 0.5174, 0.3713, 0.3543, 0.3337, 0.4146], "eot_score": 0.6319, "eot_read_ms": 29, "reference_answer": "B. This will increase GDP by $29.95.", "query": "You buy 100 shares in XYZ Corporation on the Internet and your broker charges you $29.95.\n\nA. This will increase GDP by the total cost of the shares plus $29.95.\nB. This will increase GDP by $29.95.\nC. This will increase GDP by the cost of the shares minus $29.95.\nD. This has no effect on GDP.\nE. This will increase the investment component of GDP and therefore overall GDP.\nF. This will increase GDP by the cost of the shares.\nG. This will decrease GDP by the cost of the shares plus $29.95.\nH. This will decrease the investment component of GDP and therefore overall GDP.\nI. This will have a variable effect on GDP depending on the current market value of the shares.\nJ. This will decrease GDP by $29.95.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "You buy 100 shares in XYZ Corporation on the Internet and your broker charges you $29.95. (A) This will increase GDP by the total cost of the shares plus $29.95. (B) This will increase GDP by $29.95. (C) This will increase GDP by the cost of the shares minus $29.95. (D) This has no effect on GDP. (E) This will increase the investment component of GDP and therefore overall GDP. (F) This will increase GDP by the cost of the shares. (G) This will decrease GDP by the cost of the shares plus $29.95. (H) This will decrease the investment component of GDP and therefore overall GDP. (I) This will have a variable effect on GDP depending on the current market value of the shares. (J) This will decrease GDP by $29.95. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The correct answer is B. This will increase GDP by $29.95.", "expert_answer": "(B) This will increase GDP by $29.95.", "expert_latency_s": 2.05, "stall_ms": 36, "relay_ms": 544}
{"id": "q0250", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 29.75, "n_chunks": 30, "scores": [0.4729, 0.5007, 0.429, 0.434, 0.4991, 0.5721, 0.5235, 0.4676, 0.3802, 0.3297, 0.4404, 0.3634, 0.4724, 0.5553, 0.4589, 0.453, 0.4759, 0.4039, 0.4984, 0.4164, 0.4378, 0.4958, 0.3287, 0.5681, 0.3999, 0.4664, 0.3774, 0.4496, 0.3159, 0.4299], "eot_score": 0.5982, "eot_read_ms": 28, "reference_answer": "E. all felt by a single person.", "query": "According to Parfit, both Taurek and Lewis assume that for there to be a “sum of pain,” it must be:\n\nA. all be avoidable.\nB. only felt by multiple people at different times.\nC. all be experienced in the same way.\nD. all be of the same intensity.\nE. all felt by a single person.\nF. all be caused by a single source.\nG. all be caused by multiple sources.\nH. all be caused by a single event.\nI. all felt at a single time.\nJ. all of the above.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "According to Parfit, both Turek and Lewis assume that for there to be a sum of pain, it must be (A) all be avoidable, (B) only felt by multiple people at different times, (C) all be experienced in the same way, (D) all be of the same intensity, (E) all felt by a single person, (F) all be caused by a single source, (G) all be caused by multiple sources, (H) all be caused by a single event, (I) all felt at a single time, (J) all of the above. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Parfit says that according to Turek and Lewis, for there to be a sum of pain it must all be felt by a single person.", "expert_answer": "(E) all felt by a single person.", "expert_latency_s": 6.24, "stall_ms": 28, "relay_ms": 883}
{"id": "q0258", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 42.16, "n_chunks": 43, "scores": [0.3905, 0.3764, 0.2119, 0.3388, 0.2196, 0.2686, 0.3239, 0.488, 0.2533, 0.2506, 0.2225, 0.1043, 0.3135, 0.3402, 0.2929, 0.0947, 0.2951, 0.2591, 0.312, 0.2846, 0.2882, 0.2335, 0.3299, 0.3314, 0.2962, 0.2856, 0.3827, 0.3695, 0.3506, 0.3795, 0.3264, 0.3103, 0.3422, 0.2637, 0.2986, 0.3935, 0.5083, 0.4245, 0.2896, 0.3341, 0.3805, 0.3758, 0.355], "eot_score": 0.4992, "eot_read_ms": 28, "reference_answer": "I. A tax collected at each stage of production based on the value added", "query": "What is a value-added tax?\n\nA. A tax applicable only to raw materials\nB. A tax imposed exclusively on luxury items at the point of sale\nC. A tax deducted from the total sales revenue of a company\nD. A progressive tax levied on the profits of a corporation\nE. A tax levied on the final selling price of a product\nF. A sales tax that is applied uniformly on all products at a single stage\nG. A tax only levied on imported goods to encourage domestic production\nH. A flat-rate tax imposed on individual income\nI. A tax collected at each stage of production based on the value added\nJ. A tax imposed on the total value of a product\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "i. a tax collected at each stage of production based on the value added", "mode": "local", "answer": "I. a tax collected at each stage of production based on the value added", "answer_ms": 435}
{"id": "q0269", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 91.89, "n_chunks": 92, "scores": [0.3831, 0.415, 0.1774, 0.3134, 0.3492, 0.1435, 0.5018, 0.4277, 0.3362, 0.4672, 0.3302, 0.3293, 0.2948, 0.3199, 0.1201, 0.3863, 0.1944, 0.3469, 0.3107, 0.2935, 0.4107, 0.2728, 0.2872, 0.2443, 0.2986, 0.2969, 0.2022, 0.2418, 0.1919, 0.352, 0.2962, 0.1957, 0.2786, 0.3184, 0.1895, 0.2178, 0.3521, 0.3911, 0.3095, 0.4043, 0.3131, 0.3794, 0.314, 0.2927, 0.4791, 0.3109, 0.4892, 0.3263, 0.322, 0.2619, 0.2928, 0.3523, 0.3058, 0.3671, 0.3296, 0.2827, 0.1823, 0.3728, 0.3871, 0.284, 0.4279, 0.2948, 0.2847, 0.3978, 0.2851, 0.2157, 0.3619, 0.4671, 0.3355, 0.3009, 0.2933, 0.4343, 0.4636, 0.3952, 0.3228, 0.4147, 0.413, 0.3385, 0.3401, 0.3742, 0.3915, 0.403, 0.3906, 0.3893, 0.3609, 0.324, 0.3059, 0.4473, 0.3611, 0.2805, 0.2944, 0.3715], "eot_score": 0.3568, "eot_read_ms": 30, "reference_answer": "H. B.F. Skinner's theory emphasizes the role of operant conditioning in personality development and the importance of environmental stimuli in controlling responses, implying that personality can be programmed through appropriate environmental control.", "query": "What implications does the theorizing of B.F. Skinner and otherbehaviorists have for personality theory?\n\nA. Skinner's theory focuses on the importance of self-actualizing impulses in personality development.\nB. Skinner's approach suggests that cognitive processes, such as thoughts and beliefs, are the primary drivers of personality development.\nC. Behaviorists theorize that personality is shaped by spiritual and existential factors rather than behavioral conditioning.\nD. Behaviorists like Skinner believe that personality is an innate quality and not significantly influenced by external factors.\nE. Skinner and other behaviorists argue that personality is a fixed trait that is largely unchangeable throughout an individual's life.\nF. The theory posits that personality is primarily determined by unconscious processes and early childhood experiences.\nG. Skinner's theory emphasizes the role of genetics in personality development.\nH. B.F. Skinner's theory emphasizes the role of operant conditioning in personality development and the importance of environmental stimuli in controlling responses, implying that personality can be programmed through appropriate environmental control.\nI. According to Skinner's theory, personality is the result of social learning and imitation of role models rather than conditioning.\nJ. Skinner's theory suggests that inner drives are the main factors in personality development.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "H: BF Skinner's theory emphasizes the role of operant conditioning in personality development, and the importance of environmental stimuli in controlling responses, implying that personality can be programmed through appropriate environmental control.", "mode": "local", "answer": "H. BF Skinner's theory emphasizes the role of operant conditioning in personality development, and the importance of environmental stimuli in controlling responses, implying that personality can be programmed through appropriate environmental control.", "answer_ms": 1210}
{"id": "q0274", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 18.88, "n_chunks": 19, "scores": [0.3937, 0.2539, 0.2699, 0.3246, 0.277, 0.3262, 0.3968, 0.4153, 0.4349, 0.3994, 0.3398, 0.2823, 0.4184, 0.4341, 0.4056, 0.4471, 0.347, 0.3756, 0.3414], "eot_score": 0.2867, "eot_read_ms": 29, "reference_answer": "C. LSB, Least Significant Bit", "query": "In binary number system the first digit (bit) from right to left is called as\n\nA. First Significant Bit\nB. MSB, Most Significant Bit\nC. LSB, Least Significant Bit\nD. Last Bit\nE. Binary Digit\nF. Initial Bit\nG. Binary Significant Bit\nH. Bit Zero\nI. First Bit\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "In binary number system the first digit (bit) from right to left is called as (A) first significant bit, (B) MSB (most significant bit), (C) LSB (least significant bit), (D) last bit, (E) binary digit, (F) initial bit, (G) binary significant bit, (H) bit zero, (I) first bit.", "mode": "local", "answer": "C. LSB – least significant bit", "answer_ms": 235}
{"id": "q0293", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 29.45, "n_chunks": 30, "scores": [0.4261, 0.391, 0.2932, 0.3071, 0.336, 0.3929, 0.3474, 0.3564, 0.2912, 0.389, 0.5726, 0.3996, 0.2626, 0.2501, 0.243, 0.1693, 0.3214, 0.3965, 0.3463, 0.2881, 0.3917, 0.3351, 0.3224, 0.3691, 0.3428, 0.435, 0.4364, 0.316, 0.3476, 0.4083], "eot_score": 0.4956, "eot_read_ms": 30, "reference_answer": "I. shape constancy", "query": "Young Tina had never seen the space shuttle until her parents pointed out a picture of it ready to launch. When she next saw a picture of it flying, she had difficulty recognizing it. Which concept best explains this problem?\n\nA. dishabituation\nB. sensory adaptation\nC. perceptual adaptation\nD. summation\nE. perceptual set\nF. sensory interaction\nG. binocular cue\nH. depth perception\nI. shape constancy\nJ. autokinetic effect\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "Young Tina had never seen the space shuttle until her parents pointed out a picture of it ready to launch. When she next saw a picture of it flying, she had difficulty recognizing it. Which concept best explains this problem? A) dishabituation, B) sensory adaptation, C) perceptual adaptation, D) summation, E) perceptual set, F) sensory interaction, G) binocular cue, H) depth perception, I) shape constancy, J) autokinetic effect.", "mode": "local", "answer": "E. Perceptual set.", "answer_ms": 239}
{"id": "q0308", "pool": "easy-fact", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 3.64, "n_chunks": 4, "scores": [0.5123, 0.4089, 0.3679, 0.381], "eot_score": 0.7213, "eot_read_ms": 28, "reference_answer": "Mary holding the dead body of", "query": "In Christian art what does a painting termed a ‘Pieta’ depict?", "transcript": "In Christian art, what does a painting termed a pietà depict?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "A pietà depicts the Virgin Mary mourning over the dead body of Jesus Christ, usually holding him after the Crucifixion.", "expert_answer": "A **pietà** depicts the **Virgin Mary mourning over the dead body of Jesus Christ**, usually holding him after the Crucifixion.", "expert_latency_s": 3.83, "stall_ms": 32, "relay_ms": 840}
{"id": "q0312", "pool": "easy-fact", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 3.34, "n_chunks": 4, "scores": [0.4204, 0.2879, 0.3557, 0.3526], "eot_score": 0.3817, "eot_read_ms": 30, "reference_answer": "1960s", "query": "In which decade were ZIP codes introduced in the USA?", "transcript": "In which decade were zip codes introduced in the USA?", "mode": "local", "answer": "Zip codes were introduced in the United States in the **1960s**. The first ZIP code was implemented on **July 1, 1963**, making the 1960s the decade when they were introduced.", "answer_ms": 1539}
{"id": "q0319", "pool": "easy-fact", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 6.28, "n_chunks": 7, "scores": [0.4241, 0.319, 0.3914, 0.3068, 0.2484, 0.3554, 0.3591], "eot_score": 0.4712, "eot_read_ms": 29, "reference_answer": "CATALYST", "query": "What name is given to a substance that accelerates a chemical reaction without itself being affected?", "transcript": "What name is given to a substance that accelerates a chemical reaction without itself being affected?", "mode": "local", "answer": "A substance that accelerates a chemical reaction without itself being consumed or permanently changed is called a **catalyst**.", "answer_ms": 749}
